>> title: Goma-ae
>> category: Vegetables & Sides
>> tags: japanese, sesame, green beans, side, quick, vegetarian
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: ごま和え, goma-ae, goma ae, ゴマ和え, 胡麻和え, いんげんのごま和え, ingen no goma-ae, sesame-dressed green beans
>> pairs-with: dashi, hambagu
>> servings: 4
>> time: 15 min
>> slack: narrow — sesame ground more than a few hours ahead goes stale and papery, and the beans weep into the dressing overnight; this is the one small side on the shelf that is not made ahead, and it is the one worth grinding for
>> step.1: toast 2 min, grind to a rough paste
>> step.2: blanch 3 min
>> step.3: toss through warm

Toast @white sesame seeds{3%Tbs} in a dry #frying pan{} ~toast{2%min}, shaking, until they start to pop and smell of nuts, then grind them in a #suribachi{} with @sugar{1%Tbs}, @soy sauce{1%Tbs}(15 mL) and @dashi{1%Tbs}(15 mL) to a rough paste — half the seeds broken open and half left whole, which is the texture.

Blanch @green beans{12%oz}(340 g; topped) in boiling @salted water{}(1 Tbs salt to the pot) ~blanch{3%min}, until they squeak against a tooth but bend without snapping, then drain them and cut them in half on the bias.

Toss @&(~1)beans{} through @&(1)sesame dressing{} while they are still warm. Warm beans take a sesame dressing up; cold beans only wear it.
